    "class": "Class IC antiarrhythmics (strong sodium channel blockers)",
    "class_members": [
      "flecainide",
      "propafenone"
    ],
    "mechanism": "Block sodium channels strongly and dissociate slowly, markedly slowing conduction and widening the QRS, with little effect on the length of the action potential. Propafenone also blocks beta receptors.",
    "pharmacokinetics": [
      "both are metabolized by CYP2D6"
    ],
    "indications": [
      "atrial fibrillation in a structurally normal heart, including the pill-in-the-pocket approach",
      "supraventricular tachycardias"
    ],
    "adverse_effects": [
      "proarrhythmia, especially ventricular tachycardia",
      "atrial flutter with fast 1:1 conduction",
      "QRS widening",
      "dizziness and blurred vision"
    ],
    "contraindications": [
      "structural heart disease: prior myocardial infarction, heart failure or marked left ventricular hypertrophy",
      "coronary artery disease"
    ],
    "interactions": [
      "CYP2D6 inhibitors raise levels",
      "given with an AV nodal blocker to prevent 1:1 flutter conduction"
    ],
    "monitoring": [
      "QRS width on the ECG, especially at higher doses or faster heart rates"
    ],
    "overdose": {
      "features": [
        "wide QRS",
        "ventricular arrhythmias",
        "hypotension"
      ],
      "treatment": [
        "sodium bicarbonate",
        "supportive care"
      ]
    },
    "confused_with": [
      {
        "script_id": "class-ib-antiarrhythmics",
        "how_to_tell_apart": "Class IC drugs block sodium strongly, widen the QRS and raise mortality after a myocardial infarction; class IB drugs block sodium weakly and target ischemic ventricular tissue."
      }
    ],
    "pearls": [
      "The CAST trial showed flecainide and encainide raised mortality after myocardial infarction.",
      "Pill in the pocket: a single dose of flecainide or propafenone at the onset of atrial fibrillation, with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker.",
      "Class IC drugs show use-dependence: QRS widening is worse at faster heart rates."
    ],
